      "Describe the opposite type. Of elderly men he says they have often been taken in and made mistakes, so they are sure about nothing and add a possibly or a perhaps, and tend to put the worse construction on things. {{mit-arist}}",
      "Describe the middle. Men in their prime, he says, have a character between the two, free from the extremes of either, neither too confident nor too timid. {{mit-arist}}",
      "Add fortune. Wealthy men, he says, are insolent and arrogant, and wealth becomes a standard of value for everything else. {{mit-arist}}",
      "Draw the speaker's conclusion. People always think well of speeches adapted to, and reflecting, their own character, so we can compose our speeches to adapt both them and ourselves to our audiences. {{mit-arist}}"
